// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: modules, mount, glob, name-resolution, stdlib, control
// @expected-error: E100
//
// CONTROL for `glob_mount_reaches_a_module_the_stdlib_depends_on.vr` (T0969).
//
// That spec passes if `nop` resolves under `mount core.intrinsics.control.*;`.
// It would ALSO pass if `nop` were ambient — visible with no mount at all —
// and then it would measure nothing, because its input would sit in the
// overlap between what the mount provides and what is already in scope.
//
// This is not hypothetical.  In the same investigation, a probe on
// `core.intrinsics.atomic` used `compiler_fence`, which resolves with NO
// mount whatsoever; that probe reported a working glob and had to be thrown
// away.  A second probe on the same module used `atomic_fence` — also
// ambient.  Two of the sweep's rows were vacuous before anyone checked.
//
// So this file states the other half: with the mount REMOVED, the same name
// must not resolve.  If it ever turns green, its sibling has stopped
// measuring the mount and both need re-reading — do not "fix" this one by
// changing the expectation.

fn main() {
    nop();
    print("unreachable");
}
